      <article class="article">
        <aside class="key-points" aria-labelledby="key-points-title">
          <h2 id="key-points-title">What is genuinely different?</h2>
          <dl>
            <dt>Question</dt>
            <dd>How does Simple French with Hems differ from established language products, and how could its design support children, adolescents, adults, and older adults?</dd>
            <dt>Answer</dt>
            <dd>Its most credible distinction is not any single feature. Competitors already offer AI, speech recognition, live classes, gamification, CEFR courses, or VR. The difference is the proposed <strong>integration</strong> of a French-only A0–B2/CLB pathway, diagnostics, four-skill AI practice, dictation, stories, tests, live support, and age-adapted facilitated VR in one ecosystem.<a class="cite" href="#ref-1">1</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-2">2</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-3">3</a></dd>
            <dt>Evidence</dt>
            <dd>Retrieval, spacing, corrective feedback, meaningful output, autonomy-supportive motivation, contextual immersion, and well-aligned games are supported by prior research.<a class="cite" href="#ref-34">34</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-38">38</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-39">39</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-44">44</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-51">51</a> Whether this particular combination outperforms alternatives remains an empirical question.</dd>
            <dt>Life claim</dt>
            <dd>Better French can plausibly expand participation, confidence, study, work, immigration navigation, cultural access, and social connection. The platform should not promise income, immigration outcomes, treatment, intelligence gains, neuron growth, or dementia prevention.</dd>
          </dl>
        </aside>

        <section class="section" id="summary">
          <p class="section-kicker">01 · Executive summary</p>
          <h2>Different by continuity, not by novelty alone</h2>
          <p><strong>Importance.</strong> The language-learning market is crowded with polished products. Duolingo combines adaptive lessons with AI conversation; Babbel offers expert-designed short lessons and speech recognition; Busuu provides CEFR-aligned French and community feedback; Rosetta Stone emphasizes immersion and pronunciation; Pimsleur is audio-first; Memrise pairs native-speaker video with AI conversation; Lingoda provides live teacher-led classes; and Mondly and Immerse offer VR experiences.<a class="cite" href="#ref-7">7</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-8">8</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-9">9</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-10">10</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-11">11</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-12">12</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-13">13</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-14">14</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-15">15</a> Consequently, “we use AI,” “we are gamified,” or “we use VR” is not a defensible market distinction.</p>
          <p><strong>Objective.</strong> This literature review asks a narrower and more useful question: whether Simple French with Hems has a coherent configuration of learning supports that competitors do not commonly assemble in one French-specific journey, and how that configuration could serve learners at different life stages.</p>
          <p><strong>Findings.</strong> Public materials describe an A0–B2 curriculum, CEFR/CLB-oriented progression, assessment and planning tools, Hemiya AI activities for speaking, writing, reading, and listening, Vocabuvore dictation, animated stories, quizzes and mock tests, memberships and speaking practice, plus SchoolPulse VR variants for younger learners and seated Mind &amp; Memory experiences for adults and older adults.<a class="cite" href="#ref-1">1</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-2">2</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-3">3</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-4">4</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-5">5</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-6">6</a> Prior research supports many components when each is implemented well, but it does not yet validate their combined effectiveness in this platform.</p>
          <p><strong>Conclusion.</strong> The strongest positioning is: <em>a guided French-learning ecosystem that turns diagnosis into a personalized path, connects explicit instruction to repeated real-world practice, and adapts delivery to the learner’s age, goal, access needs, and confidence.</em> The product promise should be measurable progression and participation—not vague “brain optimization” or guaranteed life transformation.</p>

