        <section class="section" id="method">
          <p class="section-kicker">02 · Review method</p>
          <h2>A structured narrative review with explicit claim boundaries</h2>
          <p>This review combined four evidence streams: (1) official product and curriculum pages for Simple French with Hems; (2) official public pages for prominent competitors; (3) authoritative standards for language levels, accessibility, and inclusive design; and (4) peer-reviewed research on immersive language learning, games, retrieval, spacing, feedback, anxiety, motivation, age, neuroplasticity, older-adult learning, and social connection.</p>
          <p>Priority was given to meta-analyses, systematic reviews, randomized or controlled studies, longitudinal neuroimaging, and official standards. French-specific research was preferred where available; however, immersive French research is sparse. A 2025 review found that only 1 of 37 AR/VR vocabulary studies targeted French.<a class="cite" href="#ref-29">29</a> Evidence from other target languages is therefore used as a mechanism hypothesis, not as direct validation of this product.</p>
          <details class="method-box">
            <summary>How claims were classified</summary>
            <div class="method-content">
              <p><strong>Verified feature:</strong> described in a reviewed official product page. <strong>Research-supported mechanism:</strong> supported by prior studies but not necessarily tested in Simple French with Hems. <strong>Platform hypothesis:</strong> a plausible advantage that requires comparative testing. <strong>Unsupported promise:</strong> a distal result—such as guaranteed employment or dementia prevention—that cannot be inferred from language-learning research.</p>
              <p>The competitor comparison is a public-feature scan, not a complete technical audit or paid subscription test. “Not identified” means a feature was not prominent in the reviewed public material; it does not prove that no version exists.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <div class="callout"><span class="label">Interpretation rule</span><p>A feature list demonstrates scope. Only learner-outcome studies can demonstrate effectiveness, and only direct comparative studies can demonstrate superiority.</p></div>
        </section>

        <section class="section" id="market">
          <p class="section-kicker">03 · Market analysis</p>
          <h2>The category has already unbundled nearly every “innovative” feature</h2>
          <p>Current competitors occupy recognizable positions. Duolingo emphasizes short, adaptive, gamified practice and AI video calls; Babbel emphasizes expert-designed instruction; Busuu combines CEFR progression with peer correction; Rosetta Stone uses image-led immersion and pronunciation technology; Pimsleur uses graduated-interval audio recall; Memrise emphasizes native-speaker video and AI conversation; Lingoda sells scheduled live classes; Mondly offers scripted VR conversation; and Immerse provides live, social VR with trainers.<a class="cite" href="#ref-7">7</a>–<a class="cite" href="#ref-15">15</a></p>

