        <section class="section" id="mechanisms">
          <p class="section-kicker">05 · Learning mechanisms</p>
          <h2>Why the integrated model could improve learning</h2>
          <h3>Retrieval and spacing turn exposure into durable access</h3>
          <p>Repeated retrieval improves long-term retention more than restudy alone, while distributed practice outperforms massed practice across many verbal-learning conditions.<a class="cite" href="#ref-38">38</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-39">39</a> More recent syntheses translate these findings into classroom practice and show that spacing and retrieval remain among the most reliable learning principles.<a class="cite" href="#ref-40">40</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-41">41</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-42">42</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-43">43</a> For Simple French with Hems, this means that a word taught in a course should recur in a story, a dictation, an AI conversation, a quiz, and a later scenario—with expanding intervals and varied cues.</p>

          <h3>Output and corrective feedback make gaps visible</h3>
          <p>Producing French forces learners to test what they can actually retrieve. Corrective-feedback meta-analyses generally find positive effects, but the value depends on timing, explicitness, target, learner readiness, and opportunity to try again.<a class="cite" href="#ref-44">44</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-45">45</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-46">46</a> The noticing hypothesis further explains why a learner must attend to the difference between an attempted form and a more accurate one.<a class="cite" href="#ref-47">47</a> AI feedback should therefore be short, actionable, uncertainty-aware, and followed by immediate re-production—not a dense correction report.</p>

          <h3>Immersion can supply context, agency, and low-stakes rehearsal</h3>
          <p>Meta-analyses report favorable average effects of VR-assisted language learning on performance and affect, while reviews also flag small samples, heterogeneous technologies, novelty effects, and uneven instructional quality.<a class="cite" href="#ref-21">21</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-22">22</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-23">23</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-31">31</a> French-specific work suggests VR can affect willingness to communicate and anxiety, but results remain context-dependent.<a class="cite" href="#ref-24">24</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-25">25</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-26">26</a> A small fNIRS pilot also illustrates why neural findings should remain preliminary.<a class="cite" href="#ref-27">27</a> Contextual encoding, interactivity, and spatial memory can make practiced language more distinctive.<a class="cite" href="#ref-28">28</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-30">30</a> The CAMIL framework proposes that presence and agency influence interest, motivation, self-efficacy, embodiment, cognitive load, and self-regulation on the path to learning.<a class="cite" href="#ref-33">33</a></p>

          <figure class="source-figure" aria-labelledby="published-figure-1-title">
            <div class="source-figure-head"><p class="figure-number">Published figure 1 · Immersive-learning model</p><h3 class="figure-title" id="published-figure-1-title">The CAMIL framework: technology affects learning through cognitive and affective mediators</h3></div>
            <div class="source-figure-media"><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="CAMIL framework diagram connecting immersion, control factors, representational fidelity, presence and agency to interest, motivation, self-efficacy, embodiment, cognitive load, self-regulation, and learning outcomes." loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div>
            <figcaption>Figure 2 from Makransky and Petersen, <em>The Cognitive Affective Model of Immersive Learning (CAMIL)</em>.<a class="cite" href="#ref-33">33</a> <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s10648-020-09586-2" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source article</a> · <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CC BY 4.0</a>. Original figure unchanged; display size is responsive.</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <p>Immersion is not automatically beneficial. Desktop or less immersive modes can sometimes work as well as headsets; seductive details and interface complexity can raise cognitive load.<a class="cite" href="#ref-21">21</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-32">32</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-33">33</a> A good scenario makes French necessary for action. A poor scenario makes the learner manage a headset while French becomes background decoration.</p>

          <h3>Games help when goals, challenge, and feedback serve the curriculum</h3>
          <p>Meta-analyses of gamification and serious games show positive average effects, but effects vary with design and comparison conditions.<a class="cite" href="#ref-34">34</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-35">35</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-36">36</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-37">37</a> Points and streaks can support initiation, yet self-determination theory predicts more durable motivation when learners experience autonomy, competence, and relatedness.<a class="cite" href="#ref-51">51</a> The platform should reward meaningful behavior—retrieval, repair, persistence, collaboration—not merely clicks or time-on-device.</p>

          <figure class="source-figure source-figure--portrait" aria-labelledby="published-figure-2-title">
            <div class="source-figure-head"><p class="figure-number">Published figure 2 · Research distribution</p><h3 class="figure-title" id="published-figure-2-title">French remains underrepresented in immersive vocabulary studies</h3></div>
            <div class="source-figure-media"><img src="data:image/webp;base64," alt="Pie chart showing English as the target language in 78 percent of 37 AR and VR vocabulary studies and French in 3 percent." loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div>
            <figcaption>Target languages in 37 reviewed studies. Figure 3 from Zhang, Hashim, and Yunus (2025).<a class="cite" href="#ref-29">29</a> <a href="https://doi.org/10.3389/frvir.2025.1522380" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source article</a> · <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CC BY 4.0</a>.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section class="section" id="ages">
          <p class="section-kicker">06 · Lifespan design</p>
          <h2>All ages does not mean one experience for everyone</h2>
          <p>Age affects sensory comfort, prior knowledge, goals, self-regulation, motor demands, social context, and preferred forms of support. Younger exposure can support long-term language development, but age effects depend strongly on input quality, intensity, continuity, and context; an early start alone is not a guarantee.<a class="cite" href="#ref-52">52</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-53">53</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-54">54</a> Adult and older brains remain plastic, and intensive vocabulary or language training can coincide with functional and structural adaptation.<a class="cite" href="#ref-59">59</a>–<a class="cite" href="#ref-63">63</a></p>

          <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Age-adapted design matrix" tabindex="0">
            <table>
              <caption>Table 2. One learning spine, four delivery profiles</caption>
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Learner</th><th scope="col">Primary value</th><th scope="col">Design priorities</th><th scope="col">Avoid</th><th scope="col">Measure</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>Children</strong></td><td>Playful sound–meaning links, stories, safe participation</td><td>Short sessions; guardian/educator mode; concrete language; repetition; readable icons; no open social contact</td><td>Dark or adversarial scenes by default, punitive failure, unnecessary data collection</td><td>Comprehension, vocabulary retention, enjoyment, return rate, comfort</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Adolescents</strong></td><td>Identity, autonomy, school relevance, peer-safe rehearsal</td><td>Meaningful choice; progress visibility; exam/school links; private practice; moderated collaboration</td><td>Childish tone, public error exposure, manipulative streak pressure</td><td>Skill growth, willingness to communicate, persistence, belonging</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Adults</strong></td><td>Work, immigration, study, travel, parenting, personal growth</td><td>Flexible scheduling; diagnostic path; authentic tasks; TEF/TCF relevance; AI plus human escalation</td><td>Generic content disconnected from goals; implied outcome guarantees</td><td>CEFR/CLB-aligned performance, task transfer, confidence, time-to-mastery</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Older adults</strong></td><td>Cognitive engagement, enjoyment, social participation, purpose</td><td>Seated option; larger text; slower pace; clear audio; facilitator; short sessions; orientation and breaks</td><td>Medical claims, forced locomotion, sensory overload, ageist simplification</td><td>Language retention, usability, comfort, adherence, social connection, quality of life</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <figure class="source-figure" aria-labelledby="brand-figure-3-title">
            <div class="source-figure-head"><p class="figure-number">Platform visual 3</p><h3 class="figure-title" id="brand-figure-3-title">A seated, guided mode changes the older-adult use case</h3></div>
            <div class="source-figure-media source-figure-media--bleed"><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Simple French with Hems promotional image of older adults in a guided session, including an older man using a virtual-reality headset, with the words Immersive, seated, guided, designed for older adults." loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div>
            <figcaption>Mind &amp; Memory promotional visual. Source: the <a href="https://simplefrenchwithhems.com/vr-french-game" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official VR page</a>, accessed August 7, 2026. The image documents intended positioning, not a clinical benefit.</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <p>Older-adult evidence requires particular caution. A randomized trial of 160 adults aged 65–75 found that eleven weeks of beginner Italian produced basic language knowledge but negligible improvement on broad cognitive measures compared with relaxation training.<a class="cite" href="#ref-55">55</a> Another randomized study found no uniform cognitive benefit, although participants with lower baseline cognition showed a possible inhibition benefit.<a class="cite" href="#ref-56">56</a> A small pilot reported feasibility and cognitive-language findings, while reviews conclude that evidence is promising but heterogeneous and insufficient for prevention claims.<a class="cite" href="#ref-57">57</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-58">58</a> VR programs for older adults may support engagement or quality of life, but cognitive outcomes vary and accessibility, cybersickness, fatigue, and facilitation matter.<a class="cite" href="#ref-67">67</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-68">68</a></p>
          <p>Accessible delivery should follow WCAG 2.2 and Universal Design for Learning: perceivable alternatives, keyboard and non-headset routes, adaptable pacing, captions/transcripts, meaningful focus order, strong contrast, large targets, and multiple ways to engage and demonstrate knowledge.<a class="cite" href="#ref-19">19</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-20">20</a> VR must be an option within the pathway, never a gate to the curriculum.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="section" id="life">
          <p class="section-kicker">07 · Better-life pathways</p>
          <h2>Language can expand participation; the platform must not promise destiny</h2>
          <p>A “better life” is not a direct learning outcome. It is a chain of contingent effects. Better French may improve the ability to participate in a workplace, classroom, service encounter, community, family conversation, trip, or immigration process. Whether those skills produce a desired life outcome also depends on opportunity, discrimination, credentials, health, resources, policy, and social support.</p>

          <figure class="figure evidence-path" aria-labelledby="life-path-title">
            <p class="figure-number">Figure 2 · Author synthesis</p>
            <h3 class="figure-title" id="life-path-title">From designed practice to possible quality-of-life effects</h3>
            <div class="pathway">
              <div class="pathway-step"><strong>Learning conditions</strong><span>Aligned curriculum, retrieval, spacing, feedback, meaningful choice, human support</span></div>
              <div class="pathway-step"><strong>Proximal outcomes</strong><span>French knowledge, fluency, comprehension, confidence, persistence</span></div>
              <div class="pathway-step"><strong>Participation</strong><span>More effective communication in study, work, services, culture, travel, family, and community</span></div>
              <div class="pathway-step"><strong>Possible life effects</strong><span>Opportunity, belonging, independence, enjoyment, purpose, social connection</span></div>
            </div>
            <div class="feedback">Evidence is strongest at the left and becomes increasingly conditional toward the right. Distal life outcomes require longitudinal measurement and cannot be guaranteed.</div>
            <figcaption>Conceptual pathway informed by learning science, willingness-to-communicate research, healthy-ageing evidence, and social-connection research.<a class="cite" href="#ref-48">48</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-69">69</a>–<a class="cite" href="#ref-71">71</a></figcaption>
          </figure>

          <div class="mechanisms">
            <div class="mechanism"><span class="index">01</span><h3>Agency and confidence</h3><p>Foreign-language anxiety can suppress participation even when knowledge exists.<a class="cite" href="#ref-49">49</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-50">50</a> Private rehearsal, graduated difficulty, supportive feedback, and choice may reduce the social cost of trying. Willingness to communicate should be measured separately from proficiency.<a class="cite" href="#ref-48">48</a></p></div>
            <div class="mechanism"><span class="index">02</span><h3>Opportunity and access</h3><p>For Canadian learners, linking CEFR to CLB/NCLC and real-life tasks can make study legible for employment, education, and immigration planning.<a class="cite" href="#ref-17">17</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-18">18</a> The service can support preparation and navigation, but it cannot guarantee test scores, jobs, admissions, or status.</p></div>
            <div class="mechanism"><span class="index">03</span><h3>Social connection</h3><p>Guided groups, intergenerational activities, and conversation can turn self-study into shared participation. Strong social relationships are associated with lower mortality risk, and social isolation is a recognized public-health concern for older adults.<a class="cite" href="#ref-69">69</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-70">70</a> These findings justify measuring connection; they do not prove that a language app changes mortality.</p></div>
            <div class="mechanism"><span class="index">04</span><h3>Lifelong growth and purpose</h3><p>The WHO healthy-ageing framework emphasizes functional ability and meaningful participation.<a class="cite" href="#ref-71">71</a> Small studies of technology-supported or dual-language training in older adults suggest feasibility and possible cognitive or psychosocial value, but results remain preliminary.<a class="cite" href="#ref-72">72</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-73">73</a></p></div>
          </div>

