        <section class="section" id="limits">
          <p class="section-kicker">08 · Limits and safeguards</p>
          <h2>A trustworthy difference includes disciplined restraint</h2>
          <p>Language learning can be associated with brain adaptation after substantial training.<a class="cite" href="#ref-59">59</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-60">60</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-61">61</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-62">62</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-63">63</a> Yet evidence for a broad bilingual executive-function advantage is inconsistent and sensitive to publication bias and study design.<a class="cite" href="#ref-64">64</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-65">65</a> Observational reports of later dementia symptoms among bilingual adults cannot establish that learning French later in life prevents dementia.<a class="cite" href="#ref-66">66</a> Simple French with Hems should describe cognitive engagement, not treatment or neuroprotection.</p>

          <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Claim language guide" tabindex="0">
            <table>
              <caption>Table 3. Responsible public-language guide</caption>
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Avoid</th><th scope="col">Prefer</th><th scope="col">Evidence needed to strengthen the claim</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>“Unlike every other app”</td><td>“Combines a French-specific curriculum, practice tools, human support, and age-adapted immersive experiences in one pathway.”</td><td>Transparent feature audit and independent comparative review</td></tr>
                <tr><td>“Proven to improve your brain / neurons”</td><td>“Designed to engage memory, attention, listening, and speech through active language practice.”</td><td>Preregistered cognitive and neural studies of the actual product</td></tr>
                <tr><td>“Prevents dementia”</td><td>“Offers cognitively engaging and socially supported lifelong learning; it is not a medical treatment.”</td><td>Long-term clinical evidence—not currently available</td></tr>
                <tr><td>“Guarantees a better job or immigration outcome”</td><td>“Builds communication skills relevant to work, study, testing, and settlement goals.”</td><td>Longitudinal, controlled real-world outcome data</td></tr>
                <tr><td>“For everyone”</td><td>“Provides multiple age, access, pacing, and non-VR routes, with suitability assessed for each learner.”</td><td>Accessibility testing with disabled and age-diverse users</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <div class="limitations"><h3>Minimum safeguards</h3><ul><li>Clear age/content labels, guardian and educator controls, moderation, privacy-by-default, and no unmonitored child–stranger interaction.</li><li>Seated and non-headset alternatives; orientation, boundaries, breaks, hygiene, motion-comfort options, and immediate stop controls.</li><li>Visible AI limitations, correction confidence, data-use disclosure, human escalation, and a way to contest feedback.</li><li>WCAG 2.2 and UDL-based testing across phones, tablets, desktops, screen readers, keyboard-only use, zoom, low vision, hearing differences, and motor limitations.<a class="cite" href="#ref-19">19</a><a class="cite" href="#ref-20">20</a></li></ul></div>
        </section>

        <section class="section" id="evaluation">
          <p class="section-kicker">09 · Evaluation agenda</p>
          <h2>The next differentiator should be unusually transparent evidence</h2>
          <p>A credible program of research should test the integrated pathway, not only whether learners enjoy a headset. The primary comparison should control for time, teacher contact, content, novelty, and feedback so that the added value of integration or immersion can be isolated.</p>

          <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Evaluation roadmap" tabindex="0">
            <table>
              <caption>Table 4. Staged evidence plan</caption>
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Stage</th><th scope="col">Design</th><th scope="col">Primary outcomes</th><th scope="col">Decision</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>1. Usability and safety</td><td>Age-stratified observation; accessibility audit; VR comfort testing</td><td>Completion, errors, help requests, discomfort, comprehension, WCAG conformance</td><td>Is the experience safe and independently usable?</td></tr>
                <tr><td>2. Learning mechanism</td><td>Randomize retrieval/spacing/feedback variants within the platform</td><td>Immediate and delayed French retention; transfer to new items</td><td>Which component causes durable learning?</td></tr>
                <tr><td>3. Comparative effectiveness</td><td>Active-control trial: integrated pathway vs content-matched conventional path</td><td>Blinded speaking/listening/writing/reading scores; retention; dropout</td><td>Does integration improve outcomes for equal time?</td></tr>
                <tr><td>4. Age-specific value</td><td>Prespecified child, adolescent, adult, and older-adult analyses</td><td>Age-relevant usability, motivation, anxiety, proficiency, transfer</td><td>For whom and under what delivery conditions does it work?</td></tr>
                <tr><td>5. Real-world participation</td><td>Six- to twelve-month longitudinal follow-up</td><td>Course completion, test attempts, workplace/community communication, social participation, quality of life</td><td>Do proximal learning gains translate into meaningful participation?</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <div class="implications"><h3>Recommended reporting commitments</h3><ol><li>Preregister primary outcomes and subgroup analyses.</li><li>Report effect sizes, confidence intervals, attrition, adverse events, and null results.</li><li>Use independent, blinded language assessments in addition to platform scores.</li><li>Publish age, access, prior proficiency, dosage, and facilitator details.</li><li>Distinguish immediate learning, delayed retention, far transfer, and life outcomes.</li></ol><p class="practice-note">The most defensible long-term moat is not a louder claim. It is a continuously improving system backed by auditable evidence.</p></div>
        </section>

        <section class="section" id="conclusion">
          <p class="section-kicker">10 · Conclusion</p>
          <h2>A strong position: one French journey, adapted to real lives</h2>
          <p>Simple French with Hems is not different because it discovered AI, gamification, CEFR, speech practice, or VR. Its plausible difference is architectural: a French-focused route that can connect standards, explicit teaching, rich practice, assessment, human support, and immersive application while changing the interface, pacing, content, and facilitation for different ages.</p>
          <p>That architecture aligns with established mechanisms—retrieval, spacing, feedback, output, meaningful context, autonomy, competence, relatedness, and social participation. It also creates a clear scientific obligation. The platform must show that its pieces are genuinely connected, accessible, safe, and more effective together than separately.</p>
          <div class="callout"><span class="label">Bottom line</span><p>The responsible promise is not “we will give everyone a better life.” It is: “we will give learners of different ages a coherent, supportive, evidence-informed way to build French skills they can use in the lives they want—and we will measure whether it works.”</p></div>
        </section>

